# TT.ESCALATION_INTERFACE -- escalate_os (S-ESC-003..008), escalate_customer (S-ESC-010..014),
# nop (S-ESC-020) and clear_customer_escalation (S-ESC-021) called directly, one open ticket per
# gate: escalation status 0 / 10 / 11 / 20, active / inactive / absent / dangling host, a
# nonexistent event id, a nonexistent ticket id. Probes the ticket rows (escalation status, date
# flags, last_public_event flag written by addevent_new) and the 60001 events the routines add.
#
# The two DEVIATION(ORACLE-BUG) sites of the translation (S-ESC-008 escalate_os, S-ESC-014
# escalate_customer: nonexistent event -> plog.err + RETURN instead of Oracle's silent
# continuation with a NULL ticket) have NO data-visible difference: Oracle's UPDATE ... WHERE id =
# NULL touches nothing and its addevent_new(NULL ticket) call swallows its own NO_DATA_FOUND. Both
# steps are therefore expected to agree ('ok', no rows) -- the correction is observable only in
# plog, which the harness does not compare.
#
# The closed-ticket path (S-ESC-009, ticket_closed propagating out of escalate_*) is in its own
# scenario, tt__escalation_interface__closed_ticket, because it carries a known transaction-scope
# difference.
#
# ORA_USER=system: the fixtures insert into stop.company, mss.host, mss.service and
# mss.host2service; TT holds only SELECT on those. Rolled back at the end (COMMITS=no).
ORA_USER=system
COMMITS=no
COMPARE_MSG=no
DESCRIPTION=escalate_os / escalate_customer over escalation status 0/10/11/20 and active/inactive/absent/dangling hosts, nonexistent event, nop, clear_customer_escalation (20 -> 0, 10 untouched, nonexistent ticket)
